    ACS-based asynchronous Byzantine fault-tolerant protocols are attractive for wide-area deployments because they guarantee liveness without timing assumptions. However, their typical two-stage structure, namely broadcast for availability and agreement for subset selection, allows a round to terminate once at least $N-f$ proposals are chosen. As a result, some honest but delayed broadcast outputs may have already consumed network bandwidth while still being discarded by the current round. This thesis studies that inefficiency and presents a cross-round broadcast reuse mechanism for ACS-based asynchronous BFT protocols in the HoneyBadger and Dumbo family.

    The proposed mechanism reuses certified but unselected broadcast outputs through payload identifiers, proof-carrying references, local cache management, and an on-demand refetch path. The implementation is built on the Honey4u prototype and includes coordinated changes across the Python ACS library and the Rust runtime: a unified ACS proposal encoding with inline payloads and references, a reusable broadcast mempool, a TOML-driven benchmark workflow, and progressively downshifted Rust ACS hosts. In the archived local multi-process evaluation at $N=12, f=3$ over eight rounds, the Python-hosted Dumbo path achieves roughly 38% to 43% end-to-end throughput gains across the tested high-load settings, while the Rust FIN-style ACS backend achieves roughly 21% to 28% gains. When normalized by bytes per delivered transaction, both backends also reduce tracked communication cost, and the mechanism remains beneficial in slow-honest and minimal boundary-fault scenarios. The results therefore indicate that the mechanism improves not only throughput but also effective communication efficiency, while still depending on reused objects that already satisfy strong availability guarantees.
